ory levels for that specific unit. Following this anomaly, the inventory levels resume a linear progression, increasing by 13 parts per unit again. Unit 78 holds 17 parts, unit 79 holds 30 parts, and this pattern continues through Record Q00153, where unit 17 holds 992 parts.\n\nA second major anomaly is observed at Record Q00154. Warehouse unit 17 (which appears to be a repeat of the unit number from the previous cycle or a new cycle starting) holds only 8 parts, breaking the previous high-volume trend. From this point, the inventory levels again follow a 13-part increment pattern, rising from 8 parts in unit 17 to 996 parts in unit 93 (Record Q00230). The final records (Q00231-Q00233) show units 94, 95, and 96", "tokens_in": 8009, "rates_measured": true, "token_events": 512, "grammar_applied": false, "decode_tokens_per_sec": 43.59205303397116, "tokens_out": 512, "finish_reason": "length", "grammar_bytes": 0, "seed": 12345, "decode_ms": 11745, "samp_temp": 0, "prefill_ms": 10681, "samp_top_k": 20}
10:35:29 [34mINFO[0m NativeInference Completion finished {"grammar_resamples": 0, "queue_ms": 102, "attempt_id": "b7b196e0ce555552d7790337c61543a4", "prefill_ms": 185, "tokens_in": 59, "prefill_ms_per_token": 3.135593220338983, "mtp_accepted": 283, "call_id": "e37a261b0dbf6a33d467f113cda685bf", "decode_tokens_per_sec": 40.70291090327868, "last_token_ms": 12841, "grammar_applied": false, "stage": "", "tokens_out": 512, "session_id": "mtp-nograph-prose-719976d6", "finish_reason": "length", "samp_presence_penalty": 0, "wire_reads": 0, "decode_ms": 12578, "mtp_drafted": 681, "samp_top_p": 0.95, "grammar_bytes": 0, "response_tail": "fy\" phase.\n\nThe system employs two models: a large, high-quality \"target\" model and a smaller, faster \"draft\" model. The draft model is typically a lightweight version of the target model, optimized for speed rather than peak accuracy. In the speculative decoding loop, the draft model predicts a sequence of $k$ tokens (a \"speculation\" or \"draft\") in parallel. Because the draft model is small, it can generate these $k$ tokens much faster than the target model could generate them one by one. However, the draft model is not trusted blindly; it is a probabilistic guess.\n\nThis is where the multi-token prediction head comes in. A standard autoregressive head predicts one token at a time based on the previous context. A multi-token prediction head, often implemented as a small auxiliary network attached to the draft model, is trained to predict a sequence of future tokens simultaneously. It learns the joint distribution of the next $k$ tokens given the current context. This allows the draft model to output a block of tokens in a single forward pass, rather than $k$ sequential passes. The head is trained using a loss function that encourages the joint distribution of the draft's predicted sequence to match the joint distribution of the target model's sequential predictions. This ensures that the draft model is not just guessing random tokens, but is learning the specific patterns and dependencies that the target model would likely produce.\n\nA critical question for an engineer is: if the draft model is smaller and less accurate, how can the final output be identical to what the target model would produce? The answer lies in the verification step. After the draft model proposes a sequence of $k$ tokens, the target model evaluates this entire sequence in a single forward pass. The target model computes the probability of each token in the draft sequence given the context and the preceding draft tokens. If the target model assigns a high probability to the draft's sequence, the", "first_token_ms": 289, "prefill_events": 0, "samp_temp": 0, "samp_min_p": 0, "samp_repeat_penalty": 1, "samp_top_k": 20, "token_events": 512, "total_ms": 12867, "rates_measured": true, "response_preview": "Speculative decoding is a latency-reduction technique for autoregressive language models, and for an...", "seed": 12345}
